A graph, kept as written
description: an acyclic containment spine, ref edges on top - cycles allowed, nothing rewritten
- >
  Containment - a key holding its value - is an **acyclic spine**: the tree. The `*` edges laid on top
  of it may point anywhere, including back to an ancestor, so the full structure is a **general
  directed graph**, not a DAG: cycles are allowed, traversal is cycle-safe, and a `*` edge is never
  expanded inline to infinity.
- >
  Containment and `*` references are **one relation kind, keyed by the child's name** - "two parents"
  is just two same-named child-edges into one node. A relation may be authored forward (`x: *target`),
  reverse (an `&` bookmark on the target), or both - valid as long as the two do not contradict. The
  engine keeps the graph exactly as written and offers a `normalize` command to reduce it to a
  canonical forwards-only form; queries see the normalized view and cannot tell which way an edge was
  authored.
- >
  A pull always yields a **shared edge, never a copy**: `*` makes two paths lead to the one
  node, and an edit through either is an edit of both.
